full_judgment:
Md. Abdul Wahhab Miah J. This appeal, by leave, has arisen out of the judgment and decree dated the 22nd day of November, 2000 passed by the High Court Division in Appeal from Original Decree No. 65 of 1997 allowing the same in part modifying the judgment and decree dated 30.09.1996 passed by the learned Subordinate Judge, 3rd Court, Chittagong in Other Suit No. 320 of 1994.&nbsp; 

 2. The respondent as plaintiff filed the suit against the defendant-appellants in the Court of Sub-judge, 3rd Court, Chittagong for declarations that the notice dated 25.08.1990 issued under the signature of defendant No. 4, the Deputy Secretary, Ministry of Industries, Government of Bangladesh, ex-officio member secretary, &lsquo;পুজি প্রত্যাহার সংক্রান্ত ওয়ার্কিং গ্রুপ&rsquo; as illegal, malafide, baseless, without jurisdiction and is of no legal effect and not binding upon it and that the entire sale price of the disinvested industrial unit Malik Re-Rolling&#39; has been paid and nothing is due from the plaintiff and also for a money decree for Tk. 39,62,121.98 as per the accounts given in schedule &#39;Ga&#39; to the plaint.&nbsp; 

 4. The debts received from the plaintiff amount to Tk. 1,35,98,874.94 as the price of the Mills, and illegally realized TK. 52,62,169.88 in excess. Accordingly, the defendants realized in total an amount of Tk. 1,88,61,044.82 from the plaintiff as the price of the Mills. On account of the current assets, the plaintiff paid to the defendants a total amount of Tk. 1,18,38,118.21. In the circumstances, the plaintiff is entitled to an adjustment of TK. 24,84,960.85 recommended by the 3(three)-member investigation committee and TK. 12,27,164.85 illegally realized as interest on account of the bank guarantee from the current assets and Tk. 11,72,969.50 realized from sundry debtors and advances to the employee, from the current assets. On adjustment, the plaintiff is entitled to a money decree for Tk. 39,62,121.00. But without giving any proper statement of accounts, the defendants suddenly served a notice dated 25.08.1990 asking the plaintiff to pay the entire balance sale price with interest within 15 (fifteen) days, in default, the entire amount would be realized as public; demands under the Public Demands Recovery Act, 1913. In the notice no amount of the so-called balance sale price; was mentioned. In the notice the defendants also threatened cancellation of the safe agreement; hence the suit.&nbsp; 

 7. The learned Subordinate Judge by his judgment and decree dated 30.09.1996 decreed the suit with cost declaring the notice dated 25.08.1990 as illegal, baseless, arbitrary and ineffective and also passed a money decree for TK. 3,59,156.00 with interest at the rate of 10% directing the defendants to pay the decreetal amount within 2(two) months from the date of the decree, on the finding inter-alia, that the plaintiff was entitled in the adjustment against its dues to Mr. defendants amounting to Tk. 24,84,960.00 as rebate as was recommended by the investigation committee, adjustment of Tk.&nbsp; 12,23,941.85 realized from the plaintiff as interest against the Bank Guarantee in the accounts of current assets, and the adjustment of TK. 11,72,969.50 which was received by the defendants from the debtors and the employees in the account of current assets. The claim of the plaintiff under item Nos. 4-6 in schedule `Kha&#39; to the plaint was, however, rejected.&nbsp; 

 8. Being aggrieved by and dissatisfied with the judgment and decree passed by the learned Subordinate Judge, the defendant-appellants filed Appeal from Original Decree No. 65 of 1997 before the high Court Division. A Division Bench of the High Court Division after hearing the appeal by the impugned judgment and decree allowed the same in part modifying the decree of the trial Court in the following terms:&nbsp; 

 9. Against the said judgment and decree passed by the High Court Division, the defendant-appellants filed Civil Petition for Leave to Appeal No. 650 of 2001 before this Court and obtained leave on 05.01.2004 to consider the submission made by the learned Additional Attorney General that the High Court Division committed error of law in taking into consideration the so-called statement of accounts to record the finding on the outstanding accounts between the parties inasmuch as the same has not been exhibited in the suit in support of the pleading or otherwise.&nbsp; 

 12. Syed Amirul Islam, learned Counsel, appearing for the sole respondent has submitted that the statement of accounts as noted in the impugned judgment and referred to by the High Court Division is not something new and the same was filed by the learned Advocate for the plaintiff respondent by way of making summary or the various documents filed by the plaintiff, which were proved and exhibited in the suit during the trial. He has further submitted that the High Court Division alter examining the statement of accounts prepared under the two heads fixed assets&#39; and &#39;current assets&#39; as well as the evidence on record found that as per the submissions of the learned Counsel of both the sides, that is, the learned Deputy Attorney General and the learned Advocate for the plaintiff-respondent, the dispute confined to 3(three) items firstly, plaintiff&#39;s claim for adjustment of taka 24,84,960.85 as rebate as found by the 3(three)-member investigation committee appointed by the defendants; secondly, the plaintiff&#39;s claim for adjustment of taka 12,23,941.85 realized as interest charged, at the rate of 13% for the excess period during which the bank guarantee was withheld and thirdly, the claim of the plaintiff for adjustment of taka 11,72,969.50 from the total dues against the purchase of the disinvested industry and the High Court Division relying on the statement of accounts with reference to the relevant exhibits rightly held that the plaintiff was not entitled to the adjustment of taka 24,84,940.85 as rebate against its dues to the defendants; the plaintiff was entitled to the adjustment to the tune of taka 12,23,941.85 realized from it as interest charged at the rate of 13% for the excess period for which the bank guarantee was withheld; Tk. 11,72,969.50 still remained unadjusted towards the total dues Of the plaintiff to the defendants and in doing so the High Court Division did not commit any error either on factor in law and, therefore, no 6.interference is called for by this Court with the impugned judgment and decree and the appeal be dismissed. 

 17. From the judgment and decree of them trial Court, it appears that it decreed the suit as a whole declaring the notice dated 25.08.1990 as illegal, baseless, arbitrary and ineffective and also gave a money decree for TK. 3,59,156.00 along with interest @ 10% directing the defendants to pay the decreetal amount within two months. In decreeing the suit the trial Court allowed adjustment of TK. 24,84,960.85 as rebate as was recommended by the investigation team towards the total dues of the plaintiff to the defendants. But the High Court Division while allowing the appeal in part disallowed the plaintiff&#39;s claim of rebate of TK. 24,84,960.85 on the clear finding that the trial Court acted beyond its jurisdiction in allowing the said claim of the plaintiff on the basis of recommendation of the investigation team as there is no provision for rebate under the terms and conditions of the deed of agreement, exhibit-&#39;9&#39;. In taking the said view the High Court Division further observed that under the law &quot;no recommendation of an investigation committee is binding upon any higher body including the body which appointed such committee.&quot; Going through the terms of the deed of agreement and the other evidence on record we find no reason to take a different view.&nbsp; 

 23. For the discussions made above, we do not find any error of law committed by the high Court Division in passing the impugned judgment and decree. Therefore, no interference is called for with the same and accordingly, this appeal is dismissed. The judgment and decree passed by the High Court Division is confirmed.&nbsp;
